{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Who has counted.</b> The wonder is that he is not reckoned among the nations, though they are a people numerous as sand; it is possible that the few are not reckoned." "<b>The dust of Jacob.</b> The meaning: that it is numerous as the dust of the earth." "<b>And the number of the fourth part of Israel.</b> The banner of Israel. One banner, in the sense of 'the utmost part you shall see, but all of it you shall not see.'" "And the translator [Targum] rendered well the Aramaic of the word ישרים, and the meaning according to my opinion is that he longed to die, and that his end be like the end of Israel, who are the portion of HaShem and not the portion of stars, for he was a diviner. And some say that the word ותהי אחריתי is doubled in meaning, and he longed to die the death of the upright, like the upright of Israel, because of his knowledge, for by the sword he shall die. And the Gaon [Saadia] said that מי מנה is the Name [YHWH], and the meaning is: You who can count the dust of Jacob, let my soul die the death of the upright. If so, what is the meaning of ומספר את רובע ישראל? And according to the opinion of many, its meaning is like ארחי ורבעי זרית, which is in the manner of רבצי, and they interpret 'the dust of Jacob' as referring to the time when he journeyed."], :book :numbers, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {3 "זרית and רבצי appear to be Aramaic grammatical terms or interpretive labels; left untranslated as their precise technical sense is uncertain. The phrase ארחי ורבעי may allude to a known hermeneutic distinction; rendered as a proper-name-like phrase without further gloss, following Ibn Ezra's terseness. The quotation in comment 2 adapts Deuteronomy 34:2, here applied to Numbers 23:10."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:19:11.665151184Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}